\details{
In a reporting tree, age groups nest: any two age groups
either do not overlap, or one contains the other.
Reporting trees arise, for instance, in census tables where
most records have detailed ages (\code{"0-4"}, \code{"5-9"}, ...),
some have only broad ages (\code{"0-14"}, \code{"15+"}), and
some have no age at all (\code{"Missing"}).

Levels are ordered as follows:
\enumerate{
\item Leaves, that is, age groups that contain no other age group,
ordered by lower limit.
\item Broader age groups, ordered so that each age group comes
after all age groups inside it, and before any age group
entirely above it. For instance, \code{"15-64"}, \code{"65+"}, and
\code{"15+"} are ordered \code{"15-64"}, \code{"65+"}, \code{"15+"}.
\item Labels that could not be interpreted, if \code{interpret_fail}
is \code{"warn"} or \code{"silent"}.
\item Labels for missing values, as defined by \code{\link[=age_is_missing]{age_is_missing()}}.
}

Different labels that describe the same age group, such as
\code{"0-4"} and \code{"Under 5"}, are placed next to each other,
in the order in which they first appear.

Leaves do not need to lie at the same depth in the tree.
For instance, a tree can have a broad group \code{"65+"} and no
broad groups at younger ages, so that \code{"0-4"} sits directly
below the root while \code{"70-74"} sits below \code{"65+"}.

\code{age_set_order_tree()} checks only that the age groups nest.
It does not check, for instance, that the leaves cover all ages.
Labels for totals, as defined by \code{\link[=age_is_total]{age_is_total()}}, are
not part of a reporting tree, and cause an error.
Some tables use \code{"Total"} to mean age not stated. In such
